"Suppose you call a friend in New York at three o'clock on a sunny afternoon in Taipei. Your friend answers sleepily, because in New York it is two o'clock in the morning. You are both talking at the same moment, yet one of you is in the afternoon and the other is in the middle of the night. How can that be?": "35ff2eff8b09",
"Suppose you call a friend in New York at three o'clock on a sunny afternoon in Taipei. Your friend answers sleepily, because in New York it is two o'clock in the morning. You are both talking at the same moment, yet one of you is in the afternoon and the other is in the middle of the night. How can that be? The answer is that the Earth is turning. It spins once a day, from west to east, so the Sun seems to rise in the east and set in the west. At any moment, about half of the Earth faces the Sun and has day, and the other half faces away and has night. Because a full turn is 360 degrees and a day is 24 hours, the Earth turns 15 degrees every hour. Long ago, every town set its clocks by its own Sun: noon was when the Sun was highest. That worked when people traveled slowly. But when trains began to cross whole countries, every station had a slightly different time, and timetables became confusing. So people agreed on standard time: everyone in a large area uses the same clock time, even if the Sun is not exactly overhead at noon. Today the world is divided into time zones. The simplest picture is 24 zones, each 15 degrees wide, one hour apart. Time is counted from the prime meridian at Greenwich, using Coordinated Universal Time, or UTC. Taiwan's national standard time is UTC plus 8 hours, and New York uses UTC minus 5 hours in winter and minus 4 in summer. Real time zone borders are not straight lines. They bend to follow the borders of countries and regions, and some places even use half hours. If you keep moving east through the time zones, your clock keeps moving forward. Go all the way around the world and you would gain a whole day. To fix this, people use the International Date Line, which runs roughly along the 180-degree meridian in the Pacific Ocean. When you cross it going west, the date jumps forward one day. When you cross it going east, the date goes back one day. So when it is Monday afternoon in Taipei, it can still be Monday morning in London, very early Monday in New York, and Sunday evening in Los Angeles. Before you call someone far away, do a little geography: find their time zone, count the hours, and make sure you will not wake them up.": "6064feb86a41",
